 ],
 "title": "Sulfur Metabolism in Yeast and Fungi",
 "summary": "This cluster revolves around sulfur metabolism in various organisms, including yeast and fungi. The gene or protein Met17p plays a crucial role in this process, as its deletion (met17Δ) leads to inefficient sulfide assimilation. The chemical homocysteine is converted to methionine, cysteine, and H2S, while H2S is toxic to C. albicans and inhibits Aspergillus niger. The gene or protein Hsu1p is involved in the reduction of sulfate to sulfide, and its expression is linked to met17Δ. The chemical NaHS is used to supplement met17Δ, and the organism BY4741 is closely related to FY4.",
